# Monte Carlo evidence

Run from the repository root with `OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 python3 code/monte_carlo.py`.
The experiment fixes 20 streams before sampling: NumPy SeedSequence entropy
20261020, spawn keys (0,) through (19,), and PCG64. Each draws exactly
1,000,000 two-coordinate uniform points. These distinct spawned streams are
used as independent pseudorandom replicates. There is no seed selection.

The timing pilot uses stream 0, which is reproduced in the main run; it is not
an additional statistical replicate or part of the reported 20,000,000-point
sample. Its timing is recorded in pilot.json. Two workers run the main study;
the pilot indicates this needs seconds, including Python startup.

per_seed.csv and summary.json contain the measured study evidence.
The summary records runtime versions, source and CSV hashes, pooled-count
and independent sample-SD checks, and theoretical binomial uncertainty.
The reported interval uses sample SD / sqrt(20) and t(0.975,19). Its coverage
is approximate, justified by large binomial counts per stream. Theoretical
uncertainty is a diagnostic, not the reported interval.

These are working evidence supplied to the report and review owners. The
objective requires only report/report.md in the assembled delivery, owned
by paper-repo; assignment.json therefore lists only that required path.

## Measured results and validation

The mean is 3.1418818; the 95% across-seed t interval is
[3.1410487102, 3.1427148898]. The signed error (mean minus math.pi)
and absolute error are both 0.0002891464. There are 15,709,409 inside
points out of 20,000,000 study points. All 20 prespecified streams are included.

The sample SD is 0.0017800516, versus theoretical binomial SD
0.0016421834 (ratio 1.083954). The mean error is 0.787429 theoretical
standard errors; math.pi lies in the reported interval. These diagnostics
show no evident inconsistency with the expected sampling variability;
they do not establish repeated-study interval coverage.

`OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 python3 code/verify_results.py` passes. It reads
the saved integer counts and recomputes the mean and sample SD using
Python statistics, checks every estimate and seed record, recalculates the
interval and errors, and verifies input/source hashes. The main run took
1.2343 seconds as measured inside the script. Timing is not scientific
output and varies between runs. Reproduction should compare integer
counts and numerical statistics, not the elapsed-time fields or CSV hash.

The pilot generated stream 0 once before the full run reproduced it: thus
21,000,000 coordinate pairs were computationally generated including the
pilot, representing the 20,000,000 points used in the requested study plus
1,000,000 repeated pilot points. Report the study total as 20,000,000.
